  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Preamble:\nYou probably have heard of that bug that leaves your avatar's skinned mesh renderer permanently disabled, but only for remote clients.\nThere seems to be some relation, as it all revolves around having no active mesh renderers, specifically linked or parented to any skeleton GameObjects.\nDescription:\nContext of humanoid avatars. When all the mesh renderers are disabled, remote clients fail to continue animating incoming parameter updates. However, as long as there is at least one mesh linked to the skeleton (as an SMR or as a child MR of any of the bone proxy GameObjects), the animator stack does not fail and keeps updating correctly.\nTemporary solution:\nBy attaching an MR anywhere in the hierarchy one can temporarily fix said issue, however this approach requires an additional SetPass to actually kick off vert/frag queue. Potentially, this could be optimized by using degenerate triangles at transform time (forcing the triangles to become degenerate in vertex program does the trick), but you can just as well discard the incoming SV stream to increase the processing time. Still, this isn't ideal.\nExpected behaviour:\nDisabling all MRs linked to the skeleton should not stop remote animator updates.\nReproducing the bug:\n1. Disable all MRs/SMRs linked to the skeleton\n2. Disable all MRs/SMRs parented to any skeleton proxy GameObjects\nRemote clients will no longer animate incoming updates.\nTo clarify: you don't have to *disable* the objects, as not having them actively queueing up (i.e. missing/disabled) for render is what causes the issue. You can just upload a skeleton with a bunch of MRs not linked to it directly, but through the constraints, and the result will still be the same as described above.\nConcrete example: hiding the body SMR by disabling it/its component.\nConcrete example: only using the skeleton for tracking point information with all MRs not being parented to the skeleton. Potentially related to animator culling?\nNot going to provide an ID, as the reproduction steps are pretty straightforward.",
